' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' json_schema/lib.mfb — the package's public API.
'
' Two steps, always in this order: `compile` turns a schema DOCUMENT into a
' `Schema`, and `validate` measures an instance against it. The split is not
' bookkeeping -- it is where the two kinds of failure are separated. A broken
' SCHEMA is a program error and fails `compile`; an invalid INSTANCE is data
' and comes back from `validate` as a list you can read, count, and report.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORT json
IMPORT collections
IMPORT strings
IMPORT regex

DOC
  PACKAGE
  DESC Validate `json::Json` values against a JSON Schema Draft 2020-12
  DESC document.
  DESC
  DESC `json_schema::compile` reads a schema -- itself an ordinary
  DESC `json::Json` value, because a JSON Schema is a JSON document -- and
  DESC returns a `json_schema::Schema`. `json_schema::validate` then measures
  DESC any number of instances against it and returns a
  DESC `List OF json_schema::ValidationError`, one entry per reason the
  DESC instance was rejected, each carrying a JSON Pointer into the instance
  DESC and a JSON Pointer into the schema.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"type\":\"object\",\"required\":[\"name\"],\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1}}}")
      FOR EACH problem IN json_schema::validate(schema, json::parse("{\"name\":\"\"}"))
        io::print(json_schema::describe(problem))
      NEXT
      ' /name: expected at least 1 characters, found 0 (minLength)
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
  DESC
  DESC Why compiling is a separate step
  DESC
  DESC Everything that can be wrong with a schema on its own is found once, by
  DESC `compile`, before any instance is seen: a keyword holding the wrong JSON
  DESC type, a `$ref` that names nothing, a `pattern` that is not a valid
  DESC regular expression, a construct outside the supported subset. `compile`
  DESC fails for those. `validate` never fails at all for a schema reason --
  DESC every outcome it has is a list of validation errors, empty when the
  DESC instance is valid.
  DESC
  DESC What it implements
  DESC
  DESC The Core and Validation vocabularies of Draft 2020-12, together with the
  DESC applicator, unevaluated, and content vocabularies' assertion behaviour:
  INFO Applicators: `allOf`, `anyOf`, `oneOf`, `not`, `if`/`then`/`else`,
  INFO `dependentSchemas`, `properties`, `patternProperties`,
  INFO `additionalProperties`, `propertyNames`, `prefixItems`, `items`,
  INFO `contains`, `unevaluatedProperties` and `unevaluatedItems`.
  INFO Assertions: `type`, `enum`, `const`, `multipleOf`, `maximum`,
  INFO `exclusiveMaximum`, `minimum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `maxLength`,
  INFO `minLength`, `pattern`, `maxItems`, `minItems`, `uniqueItems`,
  INFO `maxContains`, `minContains`, `maxProperties`, `minProperties`,
  INFO `required` and `dependentRequired`.
  INFO Identifiers and references: `$id`, `$anchor`, `$defs`, `$ref` and
  INFO `$schema`, with full RFC 3986 URI-reference resolution, so a relative
  INFO `$id` rebases every reference beneath it.
  INFO Annotations, which are read for shape and otherwise ignored: `title`,
  INFO `description`, `default`, `examples`, `deprecated`, `readOnly`,
  INFO `writeOnly`, `$comment`, `format`, `contentEncoding`,
  INFO `contentMediaType` and `contentSchema`.
  DESC
  DESC Deliberate limits
  DESC
  DESC Each of these fails `compile` rather than being approximated, so a
  DESC schema is never validated against something other than what it says:
  INFO Nothing is retrieved. A `$ref` must resolve inside the document being
  INFO compiled; one naming an external resource fails with
  INFO `errorCode::ErrNotFound`. Fetching a URL named by untrusted input is a
  INFO server-side request forgery, so this package never does it. Compose
  INFO documents yourself and compile the result.
  INFO `$dynamicRef`, `$dynamicAnchor`, `$recursiveRef` and `$recursiveAnchor`
  INFO fail with `errorCode::ErrUnsupported`.
  INFO `format` is an annotation, not an assertion. That is the Draft 2020-12
  INFO default, and it is what every other validator does unless a format
  INFO vocabulary is switched on.
  INFO A `pattern` is translated from ECMA-262 into the `regex` package's
  INFO dialect, exactly, or refused. `\d`, `\w`, `\s` and `.` are rewritten so
  INFO they mean what ECMA-262 says rather than what the `regex` dialect says,
  INFO `\uHHHH` and `\cX` become `\x{...}`, an empty class becomes an explicit
  INFO one, and a `\p{...}` naming a general category or a script is rewritten
  INFO to that name's `regex` spelling. Lookarounds, backreferences,
  INFO `Script_Extensions`, a binary property the engine does not implement,
  INFO and a negated shorthand inside a class are refused with
  INFO `errorCode::ErrUnsupported`. `\b` and `\B` are the one carried
  INFO construct: their word-character test is Unicode-aware here and
  INFO ASCII-only in ECMA-262.
  INFO Numbers are `Float`, because `json::JsonNum` is (`mfb man json`). An
  INFO integer beyond 2^53 and a decimal with more digits than binary64 holds
  INFO are rounded at parse time, so `const`, `enum`, `multipleOf` and
  INFO `uniqueItems` compare the rounded values. This is JavaScript's model
  INFO exactly, which is what the reference validators use.
  DESC
  DESC Bounds
  DESC
  DESC Evaluation nests at most 200 levels, and a `$ref` that re-enters the
  DESC same subschema at the same instance location is refused rather than
  DESC followed. The work budget is 10000 subschema evaluations plus 100 per
  DESC value in the INSTANCE, so the total is linear in what the caller handed
  DESC in: an ordinary large document validates, and a schema built to multiply
  DESC -- ten nested two-branch `anyOf`s demand 2^10 evaluations from a few
  DESC hundred bytes -- cannot buy more work than the instance is worth. A
  DESC schema is untrusted input in exactly the way an instance is, and none of
  DESC these bounds is reachable by a schema that terminates.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC compile
  GROUP Compiling
  DESC Check, index and prepare a schema document, returning the
  DESC `json_schema::Schema` that `json_schema::validate` measures instances
  DESC against.
  DESC
  DESC Compiling walks the whole document twice: once to record every `$id` and
  DESC `$anchor`, and once to resolve every `$ref` against them, check every
  DESC keyword's shape, and translate every regular expression. A schema that
  DESC survives has no remaining way to fail for its own reasons, which is why
  DESC `json_schema::validate` returns errors rather than raising them.
  ARG document The schema, as a `json::Json` value. `TRUE` and `FALSE` are schemas: one accepts every instance and the other rejects every instance.
  RET The compiled schema. Compile once and validate many instances against it; nothing in the result depends on any instance.
  ERROR 77050003 The document is not a valid Draft 2020-12 schema: a keyword holds the wrong JSON type, `type` names something that is not a JSON type, `multipleOf` is not positive, `$anchor` is not a valid anchor name, or a `pattern` is not a valid ECMA-262 regular expression.
  ERROR 77050007 The document uses a construct outside the supported subset: `$dynamicRef`, `$dynamicAnchor`, `$recursiveRef`, `$recursiveAnchor`, a `$schema` naming another dialect, or a regular expression this validator cannot translate exactly -- a lookaround, a backreference, a `Script_Extensions` or binary property escape, or a negated shorthand inside a character class.
  ERROR 77050004 A `$ref` resolves to nothing in this document. Nothing is retrieved, so a reference to an external resource reports this too.
  ERROR 77050024 The document nests more than 200 levels deep.
  ERROR 93120001 The document has more parts than the compiler will index.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET document AS json::Json = json::parse("{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"integer\"}}")
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compile(document)
      IF json_schema::isValid(schema, json::parse("[1,2,3]")) THEN io::print("valid")
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC compile(document AS json::Json) AS Schema
  RETURN compileSchema(document)
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC compileText
  GROUP Compiling
  DESC Parse schema text and compile it, which is `json_schema::compile` of
  DESC `json::parse`.
  ARG text The schema as JSON text. Reading a file is the caller's job: pass `fs::readText(path)`.
  RET The compiled schema.
  ERROR 77050003 The text is not valid JSON, or the document it holds is not a valid Draft 2020-12 schema.
  ERROR 77050007 The document uses a construct outside the supported subset.
  ERROR 77050004 A `$ref` resolves to nothing in this document.
  ERROR 77050024 The text or the document nests too deep.
  ERROR 93120001 The document has more parts than the compiler will index.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"type\":\"string\"}")
      io::print(toString(len(json_schema::validate(schema, json::parse("42")))))
      ' 1
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC compileText(text AS String) AS Schema
  RETURN compileSchema(json::parse(text))
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC validate
  GROUP Validating
  DESC Measure `instance` against a compiled schema and return every reason it
  DESC was rejected.
  DESC
  DESC An empty list means valid. The list is not truncated at the first
  DESC problem: a form with three bad fields reports three errors, so a caller
  DESC can show them all at once. Each entry carries the keyword that rejected
  DESC the instance, a JSON Pointer into the instance, a JSON Pointer into the
  DESC schema, and a sentence.
  ARG compiled The schema, from `json_schema::compile`.
  ARG instance The value to check. Any `json::Json` value is a legal instance, including `json::JsonNull`.
  RET One `json_schema::ValidationError` per reason the instance was rejected, empty when it is valid.
  ERROR 77050024 Evaluation nested more than 200 levels deep.
  ERROR 93120001 Evaluation demanded more subschema evaluations than the instance's size allows.
  ERROR 93120002 A `$ref` re-entered the same subschema at the same instance location, so evaluation could not terminate.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"properties\":{\"age\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0}}}")
      LET problems AS List OF json_schema::ValidationError = json_schema::validate(schema, json::parse("{\"age\":-3}"))
      FOR EACH problem IN problems
        io::print(problem.instancePath & ": " & problem.message)
      NEXT
      ' /age: expected at least 0, found -3
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC validate(compiled AS Schema, instance AS json::Json) AS List OF ValidationError
  RETURN runValidation(compiled, instance)
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC isValid
  GROUP Validating
  DESC Test whether `instance` satisfies a compiled schema.
  DESC
  DESC This is `len(json_schema::validate(...)) = 0` under a name that reads as
  DESC the question being asked. It does no less work: every error is still
  DESC found, it is simply not reported.
  ARG compiled The schema, from `json_schema::compile`.
  ARG instance The value to check.
  RET `TRUE` when the instance is valid against the schema.
  ERROR 77050024 Evaluation nested more than 200 levels deep.
  ERROR 93120001 Evaluation demanded more subschema evaluations than the instance's size allows.
  ERROR 93120002 A `$ref` re-entered the same subschema at the same instance location.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"enum\":[\"red\",\"green\"]}")
      IF json_schema::isValid(schema, json::parse("\"red\"")) THEN io::print("ok")
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC isValid(compiled AS Schema, instance AS json::Json) AS Boolean
  RETURN len(runValidation(compiled, instance)) = 0
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC check
  GROUP Validating
  DESC Fail unless `instance` satisfies a compiled schema.
  DESC
  DESC For the caller who wants an invalid instance to stop the program rather
  DESC than to be reported: the error's message is every problem
  DESC `json_schema::validate` would have returned, one per line. Where the
  DESC problems are to be shown to a person, call `json_schema::validate`
  DESC instead and render them yourself.
  ARG compiled The schema, from `json_schema::compile`.
  ARG instance The value to check.
  RET Nothing. Returning at all means the instance is valid.
  ERROR 93120003 The instance is not valid. The message lists every problem, one per line.
  ERROR 77050024 Evaluation nested more than 200 levels deep.
  ERROR 93120001 Evaluation demanded more subschema evaluations than the instance's size allows.
  ERROR 93120002 A `$ref` re-entered the same subschema at the same instance location.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"type\":\"integer\"}")
      json_schema::check(schema, json::parse("7"))
      io::print("valid")
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC check(compiled AS Schema, instance AS json::Json) AS Nothing
  LET problems AS List OF ValidationError = runValidation(compiled, instance)
  IF len(problems) = 0 THEN RETURN NOTHING
  MUT lines AS List OF String = []
  FOR EACH problem IN problems
    lines = collections::append(lines, describeError(problem))
  NEXT
  FAIL error(ERR_INVALID, "json_schema: the instance is not valid against the schema:\n" & strings::join(lines, "\n"))
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC describe
  GROUP Reporting
  DESC Render one validation error as a line of text: where in the instance,
  DESC what went wrong, and which keyword said so.
  ARG problem One error from `json_schema::validate`.
  RET A single line, in the form `<instance pointer>: <message> (<keyword>)`. The root of the instance is shown as `<root>`.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"required\":[\"id\"]}")
      FOR EACH problem IN json_schema::validate(schema, json::parse("{}"))
        io::print(json_schema::describe(problem))
      NEXT
      ' <root>: the required property `id` is missing (required)
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC describe(problem AS ValidationError) AS String
  RETURN describeError(problem)
END FUNC

PUBLIC FUNC describeError(problem AS ValidationError) AS String
  MUT where AS String = problem.instancePath
  IF where = "" THEN where = "<root>"
  RETURN where & ": " & problem.message & " (" & problem.keyword & ")"
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC dialect
  GROUP Reporting
  DESC The `$schema` value this package implements.
  RET `https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema`.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      io::print(json_schema::dialect())
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC dialect() AS String
  RETURN DIALECT
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC invalidCode
  GROUP Reporting
  DESC The error code `json_schema::check` raises when an instance is not
  DESC valid: 93120003, written `9-312-0003`.
  DESC
  DESC "This data does not satisfy this schema" has no `errorCode::`
  DESC equivalent, so it takes a code from generator 9, the range reserved for
  DESC codes a package defines for itself. Generator-9 codes carry no registry
  DESC meaning and are not globally unique: two unrelated packages may use this
  DESC integer for unrelated failures. Match it only around a call you already
  DESC know is this package's.
  RET 93120003.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC load(schema AS json_schema::Schema, text AS String) AS Boolean
      json_schema::check(schema, json::parse(text)) TRAP(problem)
        IF problem.code = json_schema::invalidCode() THEN
          io::printError(problem.message)
          RECOVER
        END IF
        PROPAGATE
      END TRAP
      RETURN TRUE
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC invalidCode() AS Integer
  RETURN ERR_INVALID
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC budgetCode
  GROUP Reporting
  DESC The error code raised when compiling or validating exceeds its work
  DESC budget: 93120001, written `9-312-0001`.
  DESC
  DESC Reaching it means the schema was built to be expensive -- nested
  DESC `anyOf`s multiply, and a schema is untrusted input in exactly the way an
  DESC instance is. The budget is 10000 subschema evaluations plus 100 per
  DESC value in the instance, so an ordinary large document never reaches it. It
  DESC is a generator-9 code, with the same caveats as
  DESC `json_schema::invalidCode`.
  RET 93120001.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC safeIsValid(schema AS json_schema::Schema, instance AS json::Json) AS Boolean
      LET answer AS Boolean = json_schema::isValid(schema, instance) TRAP(problem)
        IF problem.code = json_schema::budgetCode() THEN
          io::printError("that instance costs too much to validate")
          RECOVER FALSE
        END IF
        PROPAGATE
      END TRAP
      RETURN answer
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC budgetCode() AS Integer
  RETURN ERR_BUDGET
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC referenceCycleCode
  GROUP Reporting
  DESC The error code raised when a `$ref` re-enters the same subschema at the
  DESC same instance location: 93120002, written `9-312-0002`.
  DESC
  DESC That is a loop with no progress on either side, so no amount of further
  DESC work would settle it. It is distinct from the depth and budget failures
  DESC because it is proven rather than merely suspected. It is a generator-9
  DESC code, with the same caveats as `json_schema::invalidCode`.
  RET 93120002.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET schema AS json_schema::Schema = json_schema::compileText("{\"$defs\":{\"loop\":{\"$ref\":\"#/$defs/loop\"}},\"$ref\":\"#/$defs/loop\"}")
      LET answer AS Boolean = json_schema::isValid(schema, json::parse("1")) TRAP(problem)
        IF problem.code = json_schema::referenceCycleCode() THEN
          io::printError("that schema refers to itself forever")
          RECOVER FALSE
        END IF
        PROPAGATE
      END TRAP
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC referenceCycleCode() AS Integer
  RETURN ERR_REF_CYCLE
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC patternMatches
  GROUP Patterns
  DESC Test whether an ECMA-262 regular expression matches anywhere in `text`,
  DESC using exactly the translation `pattern` and `patternProperties` use.
  DESC
  DESC It answers the question a schema's `pattern` asks -- ECMA-262 defines
  DESC the keyword against an unanchored search, so `"a"` matches `"cat"` --
  DESC and it is the seam at which this package's regular-expression behaviour
  DESC can be compared against another ECMA-262 engine without a schema in the
  DESC way.
  ARG pattern The ECMA-262 regular expression, with no delimiters and no flags. It is read as though compiled with the `u` flag, which is what a JSON Schema validator does.
  ARG text The string to search.
  RET `TRUE` when the expression matches anywhere in `text`.
  ERROR 77050003 `pattern` is not a valid ECMA-262 regular expression.
  ERROR 77050007 `pattern` uses a construct this validator cannot evaluate with matching ECMA-262 semantics: a lookaround, a backreference, a property escape whose name does not carry over, a negated shorthand inside a character class, or an escape naming a lone surrogate.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      IF json_schema::patternMatches("^[0-9]{3}$", "123") THEN io::print("yes")
      IF json_schema::patternMatches("\\d", "5") THEN io::print("ascii digit")
      IF NOT json_schema::patternMatches("\\d", "\u{0660}") THEN io::print("and only ascii digits")
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC patternMatches(pattern AS String, text AS String) AS Boolean
  RETURN regex::match(text, toMfbPattern(pattern))
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC translatePattern
  GROUP Patterns
  DESC Return the `regex`-dialect spelling of an ECMA-262 regular expression --
  DESC the exact pattern `json_schema::patternMatches` hands to `regex::match`.
  DESC
  DESC This is a diagnostic, not a step a caller has to take: `pattern` is
  DESC translated by `json_schema::compile` already. It exists so that when two
  DESC engines disagree about a pattern, the translation can be read rather
  DESC than guessed at.
  ARG pattern The ECMA-262 regular expression.
  RET The equivalent pattern in the dialect `mfb spec stdlib regex` defines.
  ERROR 77050003 `pattern` is not a valid ECMA-262 regular expression.
  ERROR 77050007 `pattern` uses a construct that cannot be translated exactly.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT json_schema
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      io::print(json_schema::translatePattern("\\d"))
      ' [0-9]
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC translatePattern(pattern AS String) AS String
  RETURN toMfbPattern(pattern)
END FUNC
